---
export const prerender = false;

import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ProjectCard from "../components/contentComponents/ProjectCard.astro";
import { SITE_TITLE } from "../consts";
import { getEmDashCollection } from "emdash";

const description =
  "Description of some of the projects that Martin Driscoll is currently working on or has recently completed";
const title = "Projects";

interface TechStack {
  rendering: string;
  css: string;
  jsFramework: string;
  hosting: string;
  auth: string;
}

const { entries } = await getEmDashCollection("projects", {
  status: "published",
});

const projects = entries.map((entry) => {
  const {
    title: projectTitle,
    hero_image,
    hero_alt,
    hero_video_url,
    description: projectDescription,
    tech_rendering,
    tech_css,
    tech_js_framework,
    tech_hosting,
    tech_auth,
    lessons_learned,
    notes,
  } = entry.data;

  // ProjectCard takes a plain image URL, not the full media reference.
  // EmDash's stored local media value has no reliable top-level src/url
  // field (see the equivalent blog post migration) — resolve it via the
  // same local media base path configured in astro.config.mjs.
  const storageKey = hero_image?.meta?.storageKey as string | undefined;
  const heroImage = storageKey
    ? `/_emdash/api/media/file/${storageKey}`
    : undefined;

  const techStack: TechStack = {
    rendering: tech_rendering,
    css: tech_css,
    jsFramework: tech_js_framework,
    hosting: tech_hosting,
    auth: tech_auth,
  };

  return {
    title: projectTitle,
    heroImage,
    heroAlt: hero_alt ?? "",
    heroVideoUrl: hero_video_url,
    description: projectDescription,
    techStack,
    lessonsLearned: lessons_learned,
    notes,
  };
});
---

<BaseLayout title={`${title} - ${SITE_TITLE}`} description={description}>
  <main>
    <h1>Projects that I have worked on or am still working on</h1>
    <p>
      I have a number of ideas for things that I would like to build in this new
      world of citizen coding. I have detailed below several of them listing
      their tech stacks and current status.
    </p>
    <p>
      I have written elsewhere on this site about the difference between <a
        href="/blog/sap/opinions">facts and opinions</a
      > and how the world of JavaScript development seemed to me (as a SAPper) to
      be built on opinions. Frameworks are described as opinionated. There are books
      written on the "good parts" of JavaScript implying there are people holding
      the opinion that there are "bad parts". So I should state my opinions.
    </p>
    <h2>My opinions</h2>
    <table class="wide">
      <th>Opinion</th>
      <th>Rationale</th>
      <tr>
        <td
          >I will not knowingly code using React or anything that looks or feels
          like React.</td
        >
        <td>
          Multiple including I personally do not like Mark Zuckerberg's approach
          to life in general, I think the concept of the shadom DOM smells like
          a "bad" pattern and I tend to be a contrarian, i.e. if everyone is
          building with React then I will probably try something else.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td> Cloudflare appear to have reasonably good intentions. </td>
        <td>
          For a public listed company, at least, their position on looking to
          protect the IP of creators from AI Bots, crawlers and the like is
          (IMO) to be commended. Yes, it helps, that they offer a generous free
          tier to budding "citizen developers" such as myself. rather
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Vanilla CSS is a worthy goal but, jeez, does Tailwind save a lot of
          headaches.
        </td>
        <td>
          I remember 10-15 years ago when everything starting looking like
          Bootstrap, which was well before AI started turning everything purple
          at the end of 2025. At that time, I found <a href="https://bulma.io"
            >Bulma</a
          > as a CSS framework pleasant enough to use. Since then I have listened
          with interest to the increasing number of new "standard" CSS features that
          are making their way into our browsers and have thought "surely" now is
          the time for writing 'vanilla' CSS only. I have tried (on this site in fact)
          and let's just say 'I have enjoyed the learning experiences!' It's not the
          generalities that make it problematic but the way you hit edge case after
          edge case, which can be exhausting.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Two pizza delivery team sizes may work for making things quickly but
          it often sucks when others try to understand what has been built.
        </td>
        <td>
          Try understanding the documentation of many AWS Services (Cognito
          anyone?) and you may see that the small team deveelopment approach may
          not naturally lend itself to understandable documentation that is
          consistent across Services. There's a whole universe of conversations
          we can have about Waterfall vs. Agile development techniques but here
          is not the place to go any further.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          If there was one right way to do something then there would be one
          tool/approach/opinion on getting it done.
        </td>
        <td>
          I take the fact that there are so many opinions as evidence that there
          is rarely one right way to do any one thing. So pick a set of opinions
          that you are happy with and stick iwth it; do not get too distracted
          by all the talk of "new" this or that. After all, the only thing that
          remains the same is that everything changes. But changing everything
          at the same time is extremely hard to do.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </table>
    <div class="project-grid full-bleed">
      {projects.map((project) => <ProjectCard {...project} />)}
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  h1 {
    grid-column: measure;
  }

  .project-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    padding-inline: var(--space-2);
    width: 100%;
    max-width: var(--layout-max-width);
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  @media (min-width: 720px) {
    .project-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
      padding-inline: var(--space-4);
    }
  }
</style>
